    "questions": [
        {
            "id": "gin-q1",
            "repoId": "gin",
            "text": "How does a request travel from Engine.ServeHTTP through the middleware/handler chain to a route's final handler, and what role does Context.Next() play in driving that flow?",
            "category": "GRAPH_HEAVY",
            "goldFacts": [
                {
                    "id": "gin-q1-f1",
                    "statement": "Engine.ServeHTTP calls engine.handleHTTPRequest(c) to begin routing the incoming request.",
                    "evidence": "gin.go:672"
                },
                {
                    "id": "gin-q1-f2",
                    "statement": "Once c.handlers has been set to the matched route's HandlersChain (gin-q1-f6), handleHTTPRequest calls c.Next() to execute it.",
                    "evidence": "gin.go:722"
                },
                {
                    "id": "gin-q1-f3",
                    "statement": "Context.Next()'s loop body invokes c.handlers[c.index](c) — this is the mechanism that actually runs the middleware/handler chain.",
                    "evidence": "context.go:192"
                },
                {
                    "id": "gin-q1-f4",
                    "statement": "RouterGroup.combineHandlers appends a route's own handlers after the group's already-copied middleware (gin-q2-f4) via copy(mergedHandlers[len(group.Handlers):], handlers), producing the single HandlersChain that Next() (gin-q1-f3) later iterates.",
                    "evidence": "routergroup.go:246"
                },
                {
                    "id": "gin-q1-f5",
                    "statement": "handleHTTPRequest looks up the request path in the method's radix tree via root.getValue(rPath, c.params, c.skippedNodes, unescape).",
                    "evidence": "gin.go:715"
                },
                {
                    "id": "gin-q1-f6",
                    "statement": "When that lookup finds a match, handleHTTPRequest assigns c.handlers = value.handlers — the HandlersChain combineHandlers built at registration time (gin-q1-f4) becomes the exact chain Next() executes at request time.",
                    "evidence": "gin.go:720"
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "gin-q2",
            "repoId": "gin",
            "text": "Why does middleware attached globally via Engine.Use still run on a request that hits the 404 (no matching route) fallback path, and what recomputes the fallback handler chain to include it?",
            "category": "GRAPH_HEAVY",
            "goldFacts": [
                {
                    "id": "gin-q2-f1",
                    "statement": "Engine.Use calls engine.RouterGroup.Use(middleware...) to append the given middleware to the engine's own accumulated Handlers list.",
                    "evidence": "gin.go:341"
                },
                {
                    "id": "gin-q2-f2",
                    "statement": "Engine.Use calls engine.rebuild404Handlers() immediately after appending the middleware (gin-q2-f1), so attaching global middleware after NoRoute was already configured still refreshes the 404 fallback chain.",
                    "evidence": "gin.go:342"
                },
                {
                    "id": "gin-q2-f3",
                    "statement": "rebuild404Handlers recomputes engine.allNoRoute as engine.combineHandlers(engine.noRoute) — combineHandlers here is the method Engine inherits from its embedded RouterGroup.",
                    "evidence": "gin.go:357"
                },
                {
                    "id": "gin-q2-f4",
                    "statement": "combineHandlers copies the RouterGroup's own accumulated Handlers (the global middleware list Engine.Use appends to) to the front of the merged chain via copy(mergedHandlers, group.Handlers) — this is the specific step that puts global middleware into engine.allNoRoute.",
                    "evidence": "routergroup.go:245"
                },
                {
                    "id": "gin-q2-f5",
                    "statement": "When handleHTTPRequest finds no matching route, it sets c.handlers = engine.allNoRoute — the global-middleware-plus-noRoute chain computed above (gin-q2-f3, gin-q2-f4) — immediately before the serveError call examined in gin-q2-f6.",
                    "evidence": "gin.go:758"
                },
                {
                    "id": "gin-q2-f6",
                    "statement": "serveError calls c.Next() — the same Next() whose loop over c.handlers (established in gin-q1-f3) executes the handler chain — which is why middleware attached globally via Use() (e.g. Logger/Recovery) still runs on a request that never matched a registered route.",
                    "evidence": "gin.go:766"
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "gin-q3",
            "repoId": "gin",
            "text": "How does registering a route with RouterGroup.GET travel through RouterGroup into Engine.addRoute and the per-method radix tree, and how does Engine.Routes() read the registered routes back — including which handler in each node's stored chain it reports?",
            "category": "GRAPH_HEAVY",
            "goldFacts": [
                {
                    "id": "gin-q3-f1",
                    "statement": "RouterGroup.GET is a shortcut that calls group.handle(http.MethodGet, relativePath, handlers).",
                    "evidence": "routergroup.go:117"
                },
                {
                    "id": "gin-q3-f2",
                    "statement": "RouterGroup.handle calls group.engine.addRoute(httpMethod, absolutePath, handlers), handing the merged handlers and absolute path off to the Engine's route tree.",
                    "evidence": "routergroup.go:89"
                },
                {
                    "id": "gin-q3-f3",
                    "statement": "Engine.addRoute's route-registration step is calling root.addRoute(path, handlers), which inserts the new route into the per-method radix tree in engine.trees.",
                    "evidence": "gin.go:377"
                },
                {
                    "id": "gin-q3-f4",
                    "statement": "node.addRoute in tree.go is the function that actually inserts the path and its handlers into the radix tree used for request matching.",
                    "evidence": "tree.go:135"
                },
                {
                    "id": "gin-q3-f5",
                    "statement": "Engine.Routes() walks engine.trees via the iterate() helper to read back every registered method/path/handler as RouteInfo.",
                    "evidence": "gin.go:392"
                },
                {
                    "id": "gin-q3-f6",
                    "statement": "iterate(), the recursive helper Engine.Routes() drives, reads each route's reported Handler via root.handlers.Last() — the handler chain stored on that radix-tree node by node.addRoute/insertChild at registration time, not anything a text search on the identifier addRoute would show on its own.",
                    "evidence": "gin.go:400"
                }
            ]
        }
    ],
